LAKEFLOW CONNECT:
======================

Earlier organizations had to use lot of 3rd party tools for ingestion into lakehouse.
With Lakeflow connect we can have efficient ingestion pipelines all within databricks.

* Standard Connectors ->  ingest from cloud storage (ALDS , S3 , volumes)
* Managed Connectors ->  ingest from SAAS applications and databases.

- Batch load
- Incremental batch load
- Incremental streaming


Data Ingestion from cloud storage:
1) CTAS
2) Copy into
3) Auto loader


CTAS - Create table as Select 
========================

All the data is ingested each time. NO incremental support.

select * from 
read_files(
'<PATH>',
format => 'csv,
schema => 'order_id BIGINT, order_date STRING, customer_id BIGINT, order_status STRING, _rescued_data STRING',
header => true,
mode => 'PERMISSIVE',
columnNameOfCorruptRecord => '_resuced_data'
)


MODE: -> Defines how spark should behave when bad record in encountered
 - PERMISSIVE -> keeps job running and put bad data into _rescued_data.
 - FAILFAST -> aborts the job on first bad record.
 - DROPMALFORMED -> silently drop bad records


COPY INTO
===============
 loads data from cloud file location to delta lake
This is retryable and idempotent operation. It stores logs in _copy_into_log inside table dir.

Copy into is best suited when dealing with thousands of files.

copy into orders
from '<PATH>'
FILEFORMAT = csv
FORMATOPTIONS(
'inferSchema' = 'true',
'header' = 'true',
'mergeSchema' = 'true',
'delimiter' = ','
)
COPY_OPTIONS (
'mergeSchema' = 'true'
);


Challenges with copy into ->
 - copy into is not performant when we have millions of files coming each hour or billions of files to backfill
 - what if files are comming every 2 mins.

Auto Loader 
==============
-> more advanced technique to incrementally  and efficiently process new files.
 - It can handle incrementally both batch and stream
 - It can load files from any cloud storage.


orders_df  = spark.readStream \
             .format("cloudFiles") \
             .option("cloudFiles.format","csv") \
             .option("cloudFiles.inferSchema","true") \
             .option("cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes","true") \
             .option("cloudFiles.schemaLocation", <check pointing path>)
             .load(<path to files>)

orders_df.writeStream \
         .format("delta") \
         .option("checkpointLoation" , <check  point path>) \
         .option("mergeSchema","true") \
         .outputMode("append") \
         .trigger(availableNow = true) \
         .toTable(<table name>)


checkpointLoation helps to track schema and resume from where the job was left. Metadata for the files are persisited in RockDB,
a key value strore db.

 * When schema is evolved job fails and on retry it is success. 
 * _resuced_column contains details of the corrupt records.


We can manually define schema in autoloader. -> it will not fail job on schema evolution but move it to manually created column _rescued_data.
Give schema hints
add _metadata.file_name column


Schema Evolution Modes:
 .option("cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode", <MODE>) in stremRead

 - NONE -> does not evolve the schema, new columns are ignored and data is not rescued unless rescuedDataColumn is set. No failure.
 - failOnNewColumns -> failed on new columns and does not work unless fixed.
 - rescue -> did not evolve and bring columns to _rescue_data column. Anything that does not match structure will be moved to _rescued_data. Stream does not fail.
 - addNewColumns (Default) -> fail first time on new columns and retry fixes it. Unmatched records kept in _rescued_data column.


when you define schema NONE is default mode and when inferSchema it will be addNewColumns.
